+Ray tracing
+***********
+
+KosmicKrisp exposes ``VK_KHR_acceleration_structure``, ``VK_KHR_ray_query``,
+``VK_KHR_ray_tracing_pipeline``, ``VK_KHR_ray_tracing_maintenance1``,
+``VK_KHR_pipeline_library`` and ``VK_KHR_deferred_host_operations``. These are
+built on top of Metal ray tracing: acceleration structures are
+``MTLAccelerationStructure`` objects, and a ray tracing pipeline is compiled
+into a single MSL compute kernel holding every shader group of that pipeline,
+plus an intersection function linked into a Metal intersection function table
+to handle procedural (AABB) geometry.
+
+Because Vulkan exposes state that Metal acceleration structures do not carry
+(per-geometry and per-instance flags, shader binding table record offsets,
+BLAS handles), KosmicKrisp keeps that data in side buffers referenced from a
+driver-owned acceleration structure header.
+
+Ray tracing pipelines keep their call stack, ray payloads and hit attributes in
+a driver-owned device scratch buffer, so giving every ray in a launch its own
+frame would make that buffer grow with the launch size. KosmicKrisp instead
+dispatches only as many workers as fit in a 16 MiB scratch budget, and the
+raygen kernel loops over the launch so each worker handles several logical
+rays in turn. At least one worker always runs, so the allocation can exceed the
+budget when a single frame is larger than 16 MiB. Pipelines that declare
+``VK_DYNAMIC_STATE_RAY_TRACING_PIPELINE_STACK_SIZE_KHR`` additionally shrink
+the per-worker frame to the size passed to
+``vkCmdSetRayTracingPipelineStackSizeKHR``, plus room for the hit attribute
+prefix the traversal loop writes at the deepest frame, and never past the
+compiled layout. That lets more workers share the same budget.
+
+The following parts of the ray tracing extensions are not supported:
+
+- Host-side acceleration structure builds
+  (``accelerationStructureHostCommands``). All builds, copies and queries must
+  be recorded into a command buffer.
+- Indirect acceleration structure builds
+  (``accelerationStructureIndirectBuild``), i.e.
+  ``vkCmdBuildAccelerationStructuresIndirectKHR``. Ray tracing dispatch
+  itself is indirect-capable: both ``vkCmdTraceRaysIndirectKHR`` and
+  ``vkCmdTraceRaysIndirect2KHR`` are supported.
+- Acceleration structure and shader group handle capture/replay
+  (``accelerationStructureCaptureReplay`` and
+  ``rayTracingPipelineShaderGroupHandleCaptureReplay``);
+  ``shaderGroupHandleCaptureReplaySize`` is therefore reported as 0.
+- Update-after-bind acceleration structure descriptors
+  (``descriptorBindingAccelerationStructureUpdateAfterBind``), even though
+  every other descriptor type KosmicKrisp exposes supports update-after-bind
+  and the matching
+  ``maxPerStageDescriptorUpdateAfterBindAccelerationStructures`` and
+  ``maxDescriptorSetUpdateAfterBindAccelerationStructures`` limits are
+  non-zero.
+- ``VK_KHR_ray_tracing_position_fetch``.
+
+In addition, Metal has no notion of a portable serialized acceleration
+structure. ``vkCmdCopyAccelerationStructureToMemoryKHR`` writes a blob holding
+the Vulkan-side data only - the spec header, the per-instance BLAS handles and
+the per-geometry flags - and zeroes the region that would hold the Metal
+acceleration structure itself, since that format is opaque to the driver.
+``vkCmdCopyMemoryToAccelerationStructureKHR`` restores the BLAS handles and the
+geometry flags from the blob, and clones the remaining side tables and the
+Metal acceleration structure itself from the source, which it finds in a
+per-device table keyed on the blob's device address. The table entry is dropped
+once either the source acceleration structure or the buffer the blob was
+written into is destroyed.
+Serialized data can therefore only be deserialized from the buffer it was
+written into, while the acceleration structure it came from is still alive in
+the same process; deserializing anything else logs a warning and leaves the
+destination untouched.
+
+Known issues
+============
+
+Triangles are intersected by Metal's fixed-function intersector, which decides
+on its own what a ray passing exactly through an edge or a vertex shared by
+adjacent triangles hits. KosmicKrisp does not intersect triangles in software,
+so it cannot enforce the watertightness rules the Vulkan specification
+requires.
+
+Ray queries and ray tracing pipelines traverse through that same intersector,
+so the limitation applies to both paths: the
+``dEQP-VK.ray_query.watertightness.*`` and
+``dEQP-VK.ray_tracing_pipeline.watertightness.*`` groups are both affected.
+On the ray query side, every
+``dEQP-VK.ray_query.watertightness.singlehit.*.triangles`` case that runs on
+KosmicKrisp fails: 11 of the 12 cases, the twelfth being skipped because
+``geometryShader`` is not exposed.
+
+The only ray tracing pipeline cases exercised so far are the ten
+``dEQP-VK.ray_tracing_pipeline.watertightness.{0..9}.4096`` cases, which all
+pass. Their ``16384`` and ``65536`` counterparts are deliberately left out of
+routine runs because of their runtime, so no pass or fail count is claimed for
+the ``dEQP-VK.ray_tracing_pipeline.watertightness.*`` group as a whole.
+
 Metal workarounds
 *****************
 

